  "Number": 4,
  "Question": "A man has \\(\\$10{,}000\\) to invest. He invests \\(\\$4{,}000\\) at \\(5\\%\\) and \\(\\$3{,}500\\) at \\(4\\%.\\) In order to have a yearly income of \\(\\$500,\\) he must invest the remainder at:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(6\\%\\)",
    "B": "\\(6.1\\%\\)",
    "C": "\\(6.2\\%\\)",
    "D": "\\(6.3\\%\\)",
    "E": "\\(6.4\\%\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "For one year, interest equals the amount invested times the annual rate. The first two investments earn\n\\[\n4000(0.05)+3500(0.04)=200+140=340\n\\]\ndollars. The amount still available to invest is\n\\[\n10{,}000-4000-3500=2500\n\\]\ndollars, and it must supply the remaining \\(500-340=160\\) dollars of yearly income. Its required annual rate is therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{160}{2500}=0.064=\\boxed{6.4\\%}.\n\\]\nThe correct choice is \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
